1. What is an IP Address?

An IP (Internet Protocol) Address is a unique address assigned to every device connected to a network.

Just as every house has a unique postal address, every device on a network has a unique IP address.

Without an IP address, devices cannot communicate.

2. Real-Life Example

Suppose your friend wants to send you a parcel.

To deliver it, they need your home address.

Example:

Bilal Ahmed
Flat 302
ABC Apartment
Mumbai

Similarly, when one computer sends data to another, it needs the destination IP address.

Example:

192.168.1.10

3. Example in Linux

Check your IP address:

ip addr

or

hostname -I

Output:

192.168.1.10

This means your system's IP address is 192.168.1.10.

4. IPv4

IPv4 stands for Internet Protocol Version 4.

It is the most commonly used IP version.

It is 32 bits long.

Example:

192.168.1.10

Another example:

172.16.20.15

a) Structure of IPv4

IPv4 has 4 numbers, separated by dots.

192.168.1.10

Each number is called an octet.

192 | 168 | 1 | 10

Each octet can have a value from:

0 to 255

So these are valid:

10.1.1.1
192.168.0.100
172.16.5.25
8.8.8.8

This is invalid:

192.168.300.1

because 300 is greater than 255.

b) Why only 0–255?

Each octet is 8 bits.

8 bits can store values from 0 to 255.

You don't need to memorize binary for now; just remember:

One octet = 0–255

5. IPv6

IPv6 stands for Internet Protocol Version 6.

It was introduced because IPv4 addresses are limited.

IPv6 is 128 bits long.

Example:

2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334

Unlike IPv4, IPv6 uses hexadecimal numbers (0–9 and A–F).

a) Why IPv6?

Earlier, only a few devices were connected to the internet.

Now we have:

Mobile phones
Laptops
TVs
Smart watches
IoT devices
Cars

IPv4 addresses started running out.

IPv6 provides an extremely large number of unique addresses.

6. Public IP Address

A Public IP Address is an IP address that is accessible over the internet.

It is assigned by your Internet Service Provider (ISP).

Examples of ISPs:

Jio
Airtel
BSNL

==> Example

When you open:

https://github.com

GitHub sees your public IP, not your private IP.

==> Real-Life Example

Your home has one postal address.

Anyone in the world can send you a parcel using that address.

That is similar to a Public IP.

==> Check Your Public IP

On Linux:

curl ifconfig.me

Example output:

49.36.120.50

7. Private IP Address

A Private IP Address is used only inside a local network.

It is not accessible directly from the internet.

Examples:

192.168.1.20
10.0.0.15
172.16.5.8

==> Real-Life Example

In an office:

Computer A
192.168.1.10

Computer B
192.168.1.11

Printer
192.168.1.20

These devices can communicate with each other.

Someone on the internet cannot directly access them.

==> Private IP Ranges (Important for Interviews)

There are three private IP ranges.

Class A
10.0.0.0
to
10.255.255.255

Class B
172.16.0.0
to
172.31.255.255

Class C
192.168.0.0
to
192.168.255.255

These ranges are reserved for private networks.

==> Public IP vs Private IP

| Public IP                    | Private IP                          |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Accessible from the internet | Used inside a local network         |
| Assigned by ISP              | Assigned by router or DHCP server   |
| Unique worldwide             | Can be reused in different networks |
| Example: 49.36.120.50        | Example: 192.168.1.10               |

==> How Data Travels

Suppose:

Laptop
Private IP:
192.168.1.10

↓

Connected to Wi-Fi router

↓

Router has a Public IP:

49.36.120.50

↓

Internet

↓

GitHub Server

Your laptop communicates using its private IP within your home network, while the router uses its public IP to communicate with the internet.

